  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure exits to the east tonight and clouds slowly build\nin, but we should stay just clear enough for temperatures to \ndrop into the upper 30s and 40s once again.\n\nClouds are going to be abundant over the area by early Sunday \nmorning, but guidance continues to suggest that dry air stays \nnosed in just long enough for most of the morning to be dry. \nLate morning is when rain moves into New Hampshire and spreads \neastward across the area into the afternoon. By Sunday evening \nthe bulk of the rain is going to be south of the mountains, and \nshortly after midnight it is likely to be offshore. Similar to \nthe event that just passed, the highest rainfall totals are \nexpected to be across southern New Hampshire and into the \nsouthwest coast of Maine. This is where PWATs are going to be in\nthe 1.25 to 1.50 inch range. While we are going to be too \nstable for any convection (the early arrival of the clouds and \nrain are going to limit daytime temperatures to the 50s) \nsoundings suggest warm cloud depths are still going to climb \ninto the 10 to 12 kft range, so rain may be moderately heavy at \ntimes. As this is one of the drier areas in our CWA, it should \nbe able to handle this with no threat for flooding. Rainfall \namounts look to end up in the 0.50 to 0.75 inch range, with the \nHREF max suggesting some locations could see upwards of an inch.\nNorthern New Hampshire and the remainder of Western Maine \nlikely see lighter rain through this event, but still stand to \npick up a tenth to a quarter of an inch. The only area that may \nsee less is the Upper Kennebec Valley. As clouds clear overnight\nSunday into daybreak Monday, temperatures should fall into the \nupper 30s and 40s once again with valley fog likely forming. \nOverall this should be a beneficial rain event for our southern \nareas, but more is needed if we are going to replenish our \ndeficits. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build across Quebec on \nMonday and remain through much of Thursday. This will allow for \na prolonged period of mainly dry weather conditions and below \naverage temperatures as negative H5 height anomalies continue. \nDaytime high temperatures will generally be into the lower to \nmiddle 60s most days with overnight lows into the 30s and 40s. \nFrost will be possible, especially across interior valleys. \n\nRain chances will then increase during the Friday to Sunday \ntimeframe as a possible coastal low develops and moves northward\nalong the Eastern Seaboard. Forecast confidence at this time \nrange is low and there are significant differences in latest \nensemble and deterministic based guidance on timing, placement, \nand strength of this potential low. If it does manage to form \nand move far enough north and west, then rain, gusty winds, and \nhigh surf would be possible. Forecast details and confidence \nwill hopefully become clearer over the coming days.",
